# Samurai Linux `security` variant: the toolset that ships in the image.
#
# This list is staged into config/package-lists/ only when
# SAMURAI_VARIANT=security (build/build.sh), and live-build installs what it
# names exactly as it installs the desktop list, so a name here is a promise the
# build is held to: an unknown name fails the build, and build/verify-iso.sh
# reconciles this file against the squashfs afterwards, so a tool cannot quietly
# drop out of the image and stay in the documentation.
#
# Where the names come from is decided by etc/apt/preferences.d in this
# variant's archives directory, not by which line they are on: everything that
# exists in Debian trixie is installed from Debian, and only what Debian does
# not carry comes from the pinned Kali archive. Both kinds are listed together
# because the split is apt's decision at install time and the two archives swap
# membership between releases - a list that claimed to be "the Kali part" would
# be wrong on the first such move.
#
# What is deliberately NOT here, and why:
#   kali-linux-everything - Kali's own maximal metapackage, but it also drags in
#     kali-linux-labs (importable vulnerable lab targets). An image that ships
#     practice victims is a different product from one that ships the tools.
#   gvm / ospd-openvas - OpenVAS's postinst wants a scanner database and running
#     services; in a live session that is a download and a daemon the image has
#     no business starting, and a build that dies in a postinst teaches us
#     nothing about the toolset.
#   anything not in either archive - metasploit's `armitage` is here because
#     Kali packages it, but tools distributed only as upstream tarballs or Go
#     modules (nuclei-templates, chisel, kerbrute, ldapdomaindump, anew, gau,
#     interactsh) are not fetched. The only APT sources are Debian's and Kali's;
#     a build that downloads a raw binary from a project's web server is not
#     reproducible and not signed by anyone we have decided to trust.
#   beef-project, volatility3 - asked for and dropped, not forgotten: neither
#     name exists in Debian trixie nor in Kali's own archive, so shipping them
#     would mean building them from source or downloading a release, both of
#     which the paragraph above rules out. The names were checked against
#     dists/trixie/{main,contrib,non-free-firmware} and
#     dists/kali-rolling/{main,contrib} on the date this file was written; the
#     build re-checks them by installing them.

# --- Kali's own category sets -------------------------------------------------
# These are the membership lists Kali maintains; naming them rather than a
# thousand individual tools means the image tracks a set that is curated and
# tested upstream instead of one that goes stale in this repository.
kali-tools-top10
kali-tools-identify
kali-tools-information-gathering
kali-tools-web
kali-tools-vulnerability
kali-tools-database
kali-tools-passwords
kali-tools-exploitation
kali-tools-post-exploitation
kali-tools-sniffing-spoofing
kali-tools-802-11
kali-tools-bluetooth
kali-tools-sdr
kali-tools-rfid
kali-tools-hardware
kali-tools-gpu
kali-tools-crypto-stego
kali-tools-forensics
kali-tools-recover
kali-tools-reverse-engineering
kali-tools-fuzzing
kali-tools-social-engineering
kali-tools-windows-resources
kali-tools-reporting
kali-tools-detect
kali-tools-protect
kali-tools-respond

